---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const SampleOutput = `
==============================
   AVERY OKONKWO
   Junior
   Robotics Club
==============================
`;

const SpacingDemo = `console.log("Hello", "world");
console.log("Hello" + "world");`;

const SpacingOutput = `Hello world
Helloworld`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge: Name Card</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>
            Print a name card to the console using only <code>console.log()</code>
            and comments. No input, no variables, no math &mdash; just careful
            control of what appears on the screen.
          </p>

          <p>
            This sounds easy, and the code is easy. Getting the output to look
            exactly right is the part that takes attention.
          </p>

          <h4>Skills to Practice</h4>

          <ul>
            <li>Printing text with <code>console.log()</code></li>
            <li>Printing a blank line</li>
            <li>Controlling spacing in output</li>
            <li>Writing comments that explain sections of a program</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="the-spacing-trap">
          <h2>The Spacing Trap</h2>

          <p>
            <code>console.log()</code> puts a space between arguments
            automatically. Joining strings with <code>+</code> does not. Both of
            these are useful, and mixing them up is the most common reason a
            name card comes out looking wrong.
          </p>

          <div class="row g-3 my-4">
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={SpacingDemo}
                frame="code"
                lang="javascript"
                title="Two Ways to Combine"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={SpacingOutput}
                frame="terminal"
                lang="txt"
                title="Output"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            You will need both before you are done. Decide which one you want
            each time rather than guessing.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="tasks">
          <h2>Tasks</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Create a new program named{" "}
              <b class="text-sn-theme">cc-name-card</b>.
            </li>
            <li>
              Print a border line made of 30 equals signs. Type it once and
              reuse the same line at the bottom.
            </li>
            <li>Print your name in all capital letters, indented three spaces.</li>
            <li>Print your grade level on the next line, indented the same amount.</li>
            <li>
              Print one club, sport, job, or hobby on the next line, indented
              the same amount.
            </li>
            <li>Print the border line again to close the card.</li>
            <li>
              Add a comment above each section of your program saying what that
              section prints.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h5>Challenge (optional)</h5>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Print a completely blank line between the top border and your
              name, and another between your last line and the bottom border.
              Figure out what you pass to <code>console.log()</code> to get an
              empty line rather than nothing at all.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>Sample Output</h2>

          <Code
            code={SampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            title="Sample Output"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Your card should use your own information. The shape should match
            &mdash; same borders, same indentation on all three middle lines.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your program file. It should contain:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Five printed lines, or seven if you did the optional challenge</li>
            <li>A comment above each section</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>Check your output character by character before you submit.</b>{" "}
            If the three middle lines are not indented by the same amount, your
            spacing is coming from somewhere you did not intend &mdash; and
            finding out where is the actual lesson here.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
